{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>You have seen that from the heavens I spoke with you, etc.</b> Our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Mekhilta Yitro, section 5) that the Holy One, blessed be He, appeared to Israel at the Sea as a mighty warrior waging war, and at Mount Sinai as an old man sitting in the academy. Now the warrior in battle indicates a reddish appearance, as it is written (Isaiah 63:2–3): ״מַדּוּעַ אָדֹם לִלְבוּשֶׁךָ וּבְגָדֶיךָ כְּדֹרֵךְ בְּגַת, פּוּרָה דָּרַכְתִּי לְבַדִּי וְגוֹ׳, וְיֵז נִצְחָם עַל בְּגָדַי״, etc. And an old man sitting in the academy indicates a white appearance: ״לְבוּשֵׁהּ כִּתְלַג חִוָּר וּשְׂעַר רֵאשֵׁהּ כַּעֲמַר נְקֵא״ (Daniel 7:9). And perhaps it was in remembrance of this that you would wish to make white forms out of silver and reddish forms out of gold, in order to draw upon yourselves, by means of these forms, an influx of will from the First Cause, blessed be He. Therefore He said: ״אַתֶּם רְאִיתֶם כִּי מִן הַשָּׁמַיִם דִּבַּרְתִּי עִמָּכֶם״ — as an old man sitting in the academy, and you had already seen at the Sea the likeness of red. Therefore I need to warn you: you shall not make with Me — gods of silver and gods of gold you shall not make for yourselves." "And He said <b>״with Me״ and ״for yourselves״,</b> for the white forms of silver are in order to be ״with Me,״ for I, YHWH, teach you to succeed, and you would want, by means of this appearance, to bring the pupil close to the teacher. But the red form of gold you would make ״for yourselves,״ so that you would be saved from the One who wages war, that He not wage war against you too. But since He immediately said afterward ״מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה תַּעֲשֶׂה לִּי״ (Exodus 20:21), it appears that this is connected with the beginning of the matter, for HaShem wanted them to build an altar of earth and not of silver and gold, for one who does so is as though he made gods of silver and gold — for HaShem has no desire in the proliferation of a gift of silver and gold, but rather ״מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה תַּעֲשֶׂה לִּי,״ which indicates the trait of humility, ״כִּי בְאֵלֶּה חָפַצְתִּי נְאֻם ה׳״ (Jeremiah 9:23), and from Me they shall see and so shall they do. For in every place where you find the greatness of the Holy One, blessed be He, there you find His humility (Megillah 31a), and this indicates His great Name, blessed be He, which is composed of letters whose numerical total is the smallest of all the letters of אָלֶ״ף בֵּי״ת, when you write יוֹ״ד הֵ״א וָא״ו הֵ״א in the manner we explained above (parashat Mikeitz, 41:1). And likewise in this parashah: after He finished the Ten Commandments, which contain a number of words equal to the numerical value of עֵקֶב — and so Rabbeinu Jacob Ba'al ha-Turim explained, and he gave several explanations on the word עֵקֶב. And I say that this is an allusion to what is said (Proverbs 22:4): ״עֵקֶב עֲנָוָה יִרְאַת ה׳,״ for anyone who becomes arrogant — the wisdom of his Torah departs from him, and humility is the very thing that HaShem commanded, and it is the foundation of the entire Torah. Therefore He said: ״אַתֶּם רְאִיתֶם כִּי מִן הַשָּׁמַיִם דִּבַּרְתִּי עִמָּכֶם״ (Exodus 20:19), for ״רָם ה׳ וְשָׁפָל יִרְאֶה״ (Psalms 138:6). And even though My Presence is in the heavens, nevertheless from the place of My dwelling I spoke with you, and this is a proof that HaShem has no desire in those of lofty stature. Therefore ״לֹא תַעֲשׂוּן אִתִּי אֱלֹהֵי כֶסֶף״ etc. (Exodus 20:19), and this is a command concerning the altar, that they not make it of silver and gold, but rather ״מִזְבַּח אֲדָמָה תַּעֲשֶׂה לִּי,״ which indicates the trait of humility." "And He said <b>״In every place where I cause My Name to be mentioned.״</b> Which is composed of letters whose number is small, as stated above — then ״I will come to you,״ to one who is crushed and lowly of spirit such as yourself, as is derived in tractate Sotah (5a): ״אֶשְׁכּוֹן אֶת דַּכָּא וּשְׁפַל רוּחַ״ (Isaiah 57:15) — Rav Huna and Rav Ḥisda: one said אִתִּי דַּכָּא, which Rashi explains as: I lift him up until He dwells with Me; and one said אֲנִי אֶת דַּכָּא, meaning: I cause My Presence to rest upon him. And it stands to reason according to the one who said אֲנִי אֶת דַּכָּא, for the Holy One, blessed be He, left all the mountains and hills and caused His Presence to rest upon Mount Sinai — thus far is its language. And this is what He said: ״I will come to you and bless you,״ which is to say: אֲנִי אֶת דַּכָּא."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {1 "The prooftext from Jeremiah 9:23 is more commonly identified as 9:22–23 depending on versification; the Kli Yakar's verse number follows the Hebrew counting."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:15.634060314Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}